@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Home";
}

<section class="hero">
    <h1>DonationTracker</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        A small ASP.NET Core MVC app that records supporters and the donations
        they make, and keeps a running campaign total.
    </p>
    <a asp-controller="Donations" asp-action="Index" class="btn btn-light">View donations</a>
    <a asp-controller="Supporters" asp-action="Index" class="btn btn-outline-light">View supporters</a>
</section>

<div class="row g-4">
    <div class="col-lg-5">
        <div class="card card-body h-100">
            <h2>What it does</h2>
            <ul class="mb-3">
                <li><a asp-controller="Supporters" asp-action="Index">Supporters</a>: list, add and edit the people who donate, each with a page of their own donations.</li>
                <li><a asp-controller="Donations" asp-action="Index">Donations</a>: a paged list with the campaign total, plus add and edit.</li>
                <li><a href="/api/donations">GET /api/donations</a>: the same donations as paged JSON.</li>
                <li><a asp-controller="Account" asp-action="Login">Staff sign in</a>: anyone can view, only signed-in staff can add or edit, and every change is recorded in an audit trail.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-0">
                Every form is validated in the browser and again on the server.
                A donation cannot be dated in the future and must belong to an
                existing supporter.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col-lg-7">
        <div class="card card-body h-100">
            <h2>How it was built</h2>
            <ul class="mb-0">
                <li><strong>ASP.NET Core MVC on .NET 10</strong>, with Razor views, tag helpers and Bootstrap.</li>
                <li><strong>Entity Framework Core and SQL Server LocalDB</strong>, with the schema managed by migrations.</li>
                <li><strong>Three layers</strong>: controllers call a service, the service calls a repository, and only the repository uses the database context.</li>
                <li><strong>Business rules in the service</strong>, so they can be unit tested without a database.</li>
                <li><strong>NUnit and Moq</strong> unit tests for the service, and integration tests that run the repository against a real database.</li>
                <li><strong>Hand-written SQL</strong>: a stored procedure the app calls with a parameter, and a covering index measured before and after with execution plans (402 logical reads down to 4).</li>
                <li><strong>A paged JSON API</strong> that returns DTOs, never the database entities.</li>
                <li><strong>ASP.NET Core Identity</strong> for staff sign-in, with hashed passwords.</li>
                <li><strong>An audit trail</strong> saved in the same transaction as the change it records.</li>
                <li><strong>A CI build</strong> that runs every test on each push.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<p class="mt-4 mb-0">
    The full write-up is in the
    <a href="https://github.com/AJWCTech/donation-tracker-aspnet-mvc/blob/master/docs/DEVELOPMENT.md">development notes</a>,
    and the source is on
    <a href="https://github.com/AJWCTech/donation-tracker-aspnet-mvc">GitHub</a>.
    See also the <a asp-controller="Home" asp-action="Privacy">privacy policy</a>.
</p>
